  "structure": {
    "block_count": 56,
    "blocks": [
      {
        "block_id": "block_000000",
        "block_index": 0,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h1",
        "text": "This Is Why Your Toddler Says No to Everything",
        "text_sha256": "b782beed33033bd8d46883b22f55ebdc67d8febda6d463cd1184d701bf4e49b3",
        "word_count": 9
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000001",
        "block_index": 1,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Light copy updates and updates to sources, fact-checked for accuracy.",
        "text_sha256": "eba5039f45c1916cc2389453731fcc7b2573a3e013f9c6efa39048dc58a6b4f0",
        "word_count": 10
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000002",
        "block_index": 2,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Toddler Behavitoddler saying no, word \"no\" in pink speech bubble",
        "text_sha256": "c07b91ad7ea9c271544fa23b426d250e865d12f483069addda7b8452c42d237a",
        "word_count": 10
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000003",
        "block_index": 3,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "If your toddler won't take no for an answer — and it's the only answer she'll give — here's how to get her to do as you say.",
        "text_sha256": "8eb76552857aedc899bbcd31880a59ff6357a08134c59b6c67ef944ebbd609cc",
        "word_count": 28
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000004",
        "block_index": 4,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "When it comes to the word \"no,\" little kids can really dish it out. Perhaps when you try to make your tot toe the line, she issues a giggle (or a big fat \"no\") and blithely continues her mischievous misdeeds. Or maybe you offer your toddler her favorite story, her best-loved shirt, her must-have mac 'n' cheese, and the response you get is still exasperating: No. No. No.",
        "text_sha256": "9bb7f8890a75a59dd9738d5a356432794a0068e1c8f0bf688125cf96a3e26ffd",
        "word_count": 68
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000005",
        "block_index": 5,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "\"My daughter has probably within the last month started saying 'no' for everything,\" says What to Expect Community mom GurlMommax2. \"She’s always been pretty obedient for a toddler but lately she fights me on everything, and while I know this isn’t abnormal, I don’t know how to handle it!\"",
        "text_sha256": "92ba75216bc8640b4468dab77525546c20d0f39635f13dd86a9a240f0f2098fc",
        "word_count": 49
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000006",
        "block_index": 6,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Where did all this negativity come from? Here's why your toddler loves that two-letter word — and how to deal with her constantly saying it.",
        "text_sha256": "c3c48c0037baaaa99333fcde389b02fe6ab8f1a23bb1e1da247bea5438bfb036",
        "word_count": 25
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000007",
        "block_index": 7,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Key Takeaways",
        "text_sha256": "cc3f7f0f4513969e418b56fb93ec0876fd96dd6498395b04301f70dbddff8a45",
        "word_count": 2
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000008",
        "block_index": 8,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "“No” is frustrating for parents, but normal and healthy. Toddlers use this word to assert independence and test limits; it’s a key developmental phase that usually eases with time.",
        "text_sha256": "fe2737b772a401072e39a2ecba432f3c8a0554f60c942e30c21aff7ea8ce9072",
        "word_count": 29
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000009",
        "block_index": 9,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Do your best to reduce power struggles. Try to stay calm, pick your battles, offer your tot limited choices, avoid unnecessary questions, and don’t invite “no” when there’s no real choice.",
        "text_sha256": "74c5b040e400e3555abe0894a0d12ec546c76c91b79ccbe61cabadd286d34758",
        "word_count": 31
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000010",
        "block_index": 10,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "It may also help to make listening easier. For example, use “no” sparingly, focus on positive instructions, be consistent with consequences, explain rules simply, reduce temptations, and praise your little one's cooperation.",
        "text_sha256": "ee3ec5cb16bff3b4d219b10565036f391c99b1324f74910adb1c2a3f466c5cbb",
        "word_count": 32
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000011",
        "block_index": 11,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Why toddlers say no",
        "text_sha256": "612c9e1ddee43d4f89fe48dd52cff9f6c12d51fc9f0582fb9c5813c966abd2fb",
        "word_count": 4
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000012",
        "block_index": 12,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Yes, your child can hear you; she's just not listening. Beneath this burst of negativity are the seeds of self-expression. In fact, by pointedly ignoring your orders, she's accomplishing two of her most important goals: testing your authority and asserting her independence.",
        "text_sha256": "c5fa53744c6566ef3b3e7c0635fe482bcac42feafeba4da457d9a5868b392b8c",
        "word_count": 42
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000013",
        "block_index": 13,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "\"No\" is actually a toddler's way of laying down the law after months of letting you call the shots. Now that she can (sort of) articulate her feelings, the message is clear: Your days of undisputed power are over, Mom.",
        "text_sha256": "f8098588b8cd70f3119ffb32fa1cf7a933d1883db53357f12362cb3459a678c3",
        "word_count": 40
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000014",
        "block_index": 14,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Frustrating though it is, negativity is part of a healthy developmental phase that kids can't avoid and you shouldn't try to squash. And in truth, the battle for self-determination lasts throughout childhood into the teenage years (and often well beyond), so chances are your child will periodically push your buttons for, say, a decade or two. Trusted SourceKidsHealth From NemoursDisciplining Your ToddlerSee All Sources [1]",
        "text_sha256": "1e4d69a6ee4bdcd99edb1b3913f9849ceb7a105dbc4769dcb860bb9a7fd8a6d2",
        "word_count": 65
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000015",
        "block_index": 15,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Trusted SourceKidsHealth From NemoursDisciplining Your ToddlerSee All Sources",
        "text_sha256": "8245ec3ad964f79411924133fc5e9c70d02417da9f4fff5d7384bd0428f898bb",
        "word_count": 8
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000016",
        "block_index": 16,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Hang in there — with time, the nonstop \"no\" will yield to a more cooperative spirit.",
        "text_sha256": "d9c2e2bf9aea7fff3e5e786613749d0c216f0df37bf2344b389535d0317c7b9d",
        "word_count": 16
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000017",
        "block_index": 17,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "How to deal with a toddler saying no to everything",
        "text_sha256": "5512e7251fe24330b11e22a411e3adfeda0f6e1a78df57b3451a468f53631c54",
        "word_count": 10
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000018",
        "block_index": 18,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "If every other word out of your toddler's mouth is N-O, you can help her break the habit by trying some simple strategies: Trusted SourceAmerican Academy of PediatricsWhat%u2019s the Best Way to Discipline My Child?See All Sources [2]",
        "text_sha256": "ad386ca6c34f18fe536bc8bbf1855be1be21348ad79cf42257a66c46958597fa",
        "word_count": 38
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000019",
        "block_index": 19,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Trusted SourceAmerican Academy of PediatricsWhat%u2019s the Best Way to Discipline My Child?See All Sources",
        "text_sha256": "e7e75824649bbf27f2f155af40ed160d57e43a36f6ffbe84da95f0cd2170a1ca",
        "word_count": 14
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000020",
        "block_index": 20,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "Play it cool",
        "text_sha256": "c07c1b463515e21682651279aae104f03e3738efb28f33745235441b240778f0",
        "word_count": 3
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000021",
        "block_index": 21,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Make a real effort not to let this behavior get to you. When she shouts \"no!\" it can be tempting to shout back \"yes!\" But getting riled up will only fuel her fire (and raise your blood pressure).",
        "text_sha256": "9d973e022f8f5f23ff113d52fba54917d63cce077d1229a8ad88022e672e8d91",
        "word_count": 38
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000022",
        "block_index": 22,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Stay calm and use reason: Explain that while you understand her feelings, sometimes she simply has to do what you say — even if she doesn't want to.",
        "text_sha256": "7032a6c137e893536f8c63904a53f80901e6b6f58afb7c0ada8b41853e5772cc",
        "word_count": 28
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000023",
        "block_index": 23,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "Pick your battles",
        "text_sha256": "070e48cf53a5f07fa7b56f83ecfc54b1d3023c5b95b834173185424f116cb231",
        "word_count": 3
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000024",
        "block_index": 24,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Do try to say yes to \"no\" sometimes. Yielding power can be just as important as wielding it.",
        "text_sha256": "1bfe5465b4752891bcf8309930c77027dd08111d3f35385bbc62f897b25bb0aa",
        "word_count": 18
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000025",
        "block_index": 25,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "If your toddler says \"no\" when the stakes are low, consider capitulating to keep the peace and let her have the satisfaction of calling the shots. For example, if you want her to wear the pink socks but she insists on the red, let her have her way. (But don't give in during a temper tantrum — that will only encourage future meltdowns.) Trusted SourceAmerican Academy of PediatricsTop Tips for Surviving TantrumsSee All Sources [3]",
        "text_sha256": "0fde8551c2043b1819927cd2142e3b5b9b3b8a69d8be27cff7bf6460a33dd716",
        "word_count": 75
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000026",
        "block_index": 26,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Trusted SourceAmerican Academy of PediatricsTop Tips for Surviving TantrumsSee All Sources",
        "text_sha256": "3f75de0ec75eb87181c4d24bc803c970d465465eb65ff4de7fbe8cc42dd1ebd5",
        "word_count": 11
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000027",
        "block_index": 27,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "Minimize opportunities for \"no\"",
        "text_sha256": "41fe21535653abfb805dfda187e1a2eeb4683858c7bfcacec38988c27047df93",
        "word_count": 4
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000028",
        "block_index": 28,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Cut down on her chances to refuse by casting your questions carefully. Instead of asking, \"Would you like green beans tonight?\" offer a choice: \"Do you want green beans or peas?\" Let her participate in the decision-making process, and she'll feel more in charge of her dinner — and her destiny.",
        "text_sha256": "158b81b9c2dc02ce53ac2094c1a5daafcf19ad8895af81a4001d9daa9aeed8af",
        "word_count": 51
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000029",
        "block_index": 29,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "Don't offer choices when they don't exist",
        "text_sha256": "9d792e9236b8a5568b6b9c10f43a6a5bbcdcc95403548bf89733a0a0115d7467",
        "word_count": 7
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000030",
        "block_index": 30,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Avoid asking your child to weigh in when the issue is non-negotiable. For example, saying \"How about if we go to the doctor today?\" is likely to spawn a mini-mutiny. In cases when your tot's got no choice, it's best just to tell it like it is. (\"Today we're going to the doctor.\")",
        "text_sha256": "7724be756d2bbebac0acdf23d3a59347f7bdd12c027e7bcd84d48f678a37ee86",
        "word_count": 53
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000031",
        "block_index": 31,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "Bypass bossy behavior",
        "text_sha256": "b6a8c2d98ef291d654976c6586a7c68d20d8b6995ba90e0ed5299cd6abb68f2c",
        "word_count": 3
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000032",
        "block_index": 32,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Nobody enjoys being ordered around — least of all a headstrong toddler . Instead, try to make your child a partner in tackling tasks.",
        "text_sha256": "549e30023f3909638ed035a9a23a2b2f29d339246ce784d7a0e149e1531967a0",
        "word_count": 24
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000033",
        "block_index": 33,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "If you're trying to get her buckled into her car seat, for example, say, \"Okay, here's the car. What do we do next?\" That way, she'll focus on your shared goal and (just maybe) skip the power struggle.",
        "text_sha256": "6185af4bcc7793146d4f17ff797df3d37699d61fcce9684111a56cf2516ab5f0",
        "word_count": 38
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000034",
        "block_index": 34,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "Use \"no\" selectively",
        "text_sha256": "ffc07287dd8be9644025b3e93c63c4a3bd7218e5c87df2ea90208dde295e9e8e",
        "word_count": 3
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000035",
        "block_index": 35,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Many parents don't realize how often they end up saying no: \"No playing in the dining room.\" \"No touching Mommy's phone.\" \"No cookies.\" A tot who hears the word all the time will learn to say \"no\" right back to you.",
        "text_sha256": "b8cfec746580dcdea80e2d39b766e44bb9308c3f649c6b6f1f172dd0c31e0fa2",
        "word_count": 41
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000036",
        "block_index": 36,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Have more questions about other tricky toddler behaviors? The What to Expect app is your guide to the toddler years, with expert-backed articles and tips from other parents.",
        "text_sha256": "dd7fd7fcc28bf36238dde4707b04220b2275eaf3a8b1eac7c5269e5545f522ea",
        "word_count": 28
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000037",
        "block_index": 37,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "How to get your toddler to listen to you",
        "text_sha256": "4b0d9132f1c6c1f0cb0c0e98a674495acde9d5c38471c6e7ca998c25302fd452",
        "word_count": 9
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000038",
        "block_index": 38,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "If you find that you're also saying \"no\" all the time, you might realize that the word is losing some of its power. Reduce the number of times you'll need to say it by trying the following:",
        "text_sha256": "7554b5b1e44165648d013ff798392b08e75746b089b476f62cfa5a371b0ffb31",
        "word_count": 37
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000039",
        "block_index": 39,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "Focus on the positive",
        "text_sha256": "cab01676400c845cb9698ed9114e25b276d95747a7759ff4bf025aa13a45370e",
        "word_count": 4
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000040",
        "block_index": 40,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "If possible, think before you say \"no,\" use it sparingly, or try to avoid it altogether by turning a negative into a positive. Talk to your toddler about what you expect her to do (\"Please stay on the sidewalk\") rather than what's forbidden (\"Don't stomp in the mud\"). This will make off-limits activities (a little) less tempting.",
        "text_sha256": "b58dcd2dc156f00721441e33fd8b2ce6677f1dce5e75b83692bb3f4d42d4d5ed",
        "word_count": 57
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000041",
        "block_index": 41,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "You can also offer alternatives: For example, say, \"You can play in the playroom instead of the dining room\" or \"You can't have a cookie but you can have a piece of melon.\"",
        "text_sha256": "9873b7717049e7134ce5e39a6cc2c783356865f521d03d873a7eca123f5fbfeb",
        "word_count": 33
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000042",
        "block_index": 42,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "Say \"no\" like you mean it",
        "text_sha256": "a9a333b206cd3e0aae92bf83b8cf0dff0fce68d5fc5607215a8c4203d5bbf7f8",
        "word_count": 6
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000043",
        "block_index": 43,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "When you do need to say it, use a tone of voice to match. It's all too easy to laugh at a toddler's rascally antics, but if she thinks you don't take your rules seriously, neither will she.",
        "text_sha256": "a740f0f0891201f28553d71ab0ee97d1dae8fd01109e6a149c5d6b834b55e095",
        "word_count": 38
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000044",
        "block_index": 44,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "Be consistent about consequences",
        "text_sha256": "c01985412635f159aa65e1e38873762a20bd268362f096b1d80f6d1e96436858",
        "word_count": 4
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000045",
        "block_index": 45,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Back up your rules with definitive action should they be broken. If you tell your tot to stay out of your dresser and she rummages through your drawers anyway, immediately follow through with a straightforward penalty — tell her you're upset she didn't listen and remove her from the room.",
        "text_sha256": "fb458090c2d7aae35722a2c2b6f84a90b9a7396197773123761e38360436cff2",
        "word_count": 50
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000046",
        "block_index": 46,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "Explain the reasons behind the rules",
        "text_sha256": "83fa52c6931415534328921d763157a24c4744ab5b1050ccebc6b800ee1947f5",
        "word_count": 6
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000047",
        "block_index": 47,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "If kids understand why a rule exists, they may find it easier to follow. Keep it simple: \"Stay away from the stove because it's very hot and can burn you.\" \"Don't climb on the bookcase; it could tip over on top of you.\"",
        "text_sha256": "4fe4a88ecb81bb2b8c3b3a1f73307d423e6e18e745c38505dceb9b33b6c8a5b1",
        "word_count": 43
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000048",
        "block_index": 48,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "Don't scold too soon",
        "text_sha256": "e8be253574fa247db4cd5bc61cd823cb109d740cb9eb43d85e327f2647c50129",
        "word_count": 4
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000049",
        "block_index": 49,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "If you forecast misdeeds and admonish prematurely (for example, you say, \"Don't touch the iPad!\" when she's merely looking at it), she'll be more inclined to break the rules than if you'd said nothing at all. After all, if you react so strongly, whatever it is must be worth touching.",
        "text_sha256": "89b283b50d5411214a3ff647a4955261e9a1ae7d4b325d658dbb64b80b323b78",
        "word_count": 50
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000050",
        "block_index": 50,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "Reduce temptations",
        "text_sha256": "a6afd7e22ec97ca05ece983ef587674d95d30387b920ca90def1b44f731435ab",
        "word_count": 2
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000051",
        "block_index": 51,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "While some things exceed your control, arrange her environment in ways that maximize her freedom when possible. For example, store tempting items (a countertop cookie jar, verboten electronics … ) out of sight and out of reach.",
        "text_sha256": "7efdb393d9de0bbbbb701aef7ef64cdb5a30cbc242b4d0a885e4d3f7bbd18cfb",
        "word_count": 37
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000052",
        "block_index": 52,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "Encourage self-control",
        "text_sha256": "e2f530b968846fd7ddf5628e858d5e2d1ea24d1064b17ade2a1808a46396a4f6",
        "word_count": 2
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000053",
        "block_index": 53,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Try granting your toddler limited access to certain objects (\"It's okay to use these special cups for your restaurant, but only if I'm here with you\") and see how she handles it.",
        "text_sha256": "cc53a0501a46c721c9b61d25398a38e259db82a550c4ad66c708828adbf4f67d",
        "word_count": 32
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000054",
        "block_index": 54,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "Commend compliance",
        "text_sha256": "c81e443b396f023acec9256007f275d7c892a96e2a8b18cd13599e743cd14228",
        "word_count": 2
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000055",
        "block_index": 55,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "In the (rare) event that your toddler follows directions on the first try, take time to acknowledge that. (\"Thanks for putting your shoes on, buddy.\") A quick pat on the back will motivate her to cooperate in the future. And by the way, that works with grown-ups too.",
        "text_sha256": "21abf59383e56e58d8a092cc29b3eddcea4ca6fa0f68f6b0b4aa3863f45a03a0",
        "word_count": 48
      }
    ],
    "content_word_count": 1422,
    "heading_count": 19
  },
  "title": "This Is Why Your Toddler Says No to Everything",
